stages:
  4 aggregation optics — Kaleidoscope (aggregate through an
    Applicative) and the algebraic/classifying lens (put is an
    algebra). Clarke-Elkins-Gibbons-Loregian-Milewski-Pillmore-Roman
    2024. okay already has the machine: Aggregator is a Moore machine,
    zip is the applicative, merge the semigroup, present the algebra.
  5 Scan as an arrow — okay-lex's Scan IS a Mealy machine, and a
    Mealy machine is Category + Arrow. Sequential and parallel
    composition of scanners, which today is impossible.
  6 the form path lawfully — `at` composed with `non(default)` makes
    "create the missing parent" a LAWFUL optic instead of the
    unlawful lens Form.edit hides. Then Form.edit is leaf
    interpretation, not navigation.
  7 textbook — ch.10 gains: aggregation optics, arrows/monoids
    (Rivas-Jaskelioff), origami and recursion schemes (Uniplate's
    plate is a traversal; the bottom-up rewrite is a fold OVER it,
    which is okay's own finding), the zipper as the optic's residual
    (McBride's derivative), and a real bibliography.

speed (the operator's question, answered with a plan not a claim):
  Fuse already reaches hand-written to the byte for set/modify. What
  is NOT covered: get/fold, optics built by the Focus macro, and the
  law-licensed rewrites that would beat hand-written code. Measured
  or refuted, never asserted.

gate: full matrix at the end of the lane, one gate for the arc.
